{
  "description": "Issues #979 and #980, the execution half. Two readers did not honour `spec/grammar.md:34`'s \"within a single expression ... newlines are insignificant\": the `value`/`of` interception of the Heritage `value of ... for key ...` reader (#979), and the `.field` postfix (#980). Measured against the built parser at `ca653709`, before the fix: `print value` + newline + `of :d for key :k` raised `ol-bad-token` four times, and `print ( :d` + newline + `.a )` raised `ol-unmatched-paren` TWICE on parentheses that are correctly matched. The companion check fixture proves these spellings are no longer DIAGNOSED; this one proves they MEAN what their one-line spellings mean, which no diagnostic can show. That distinction is load-bearing here: #980's defect dropped the `.field` entirely and left a bare variable read -- a SMALLER tree, not a malformed one, so the program still ran and printed the whole dictionary instead of the field. Every spelling below is therefore followed by its one-line twin and the two `print` payloads of a pair are identical; the exact trees are compared directly in `packages/parser/src/newline-continuations-and-delimiters.test.mjs`. The `instruction` spans deliberately differ within a pair, since the newline spelling really does span more source lines, so what is asserted here is identity of the VALUES. The axes are varied on purpose rather than sampled -- container (bare, paren, list, dict), newline position, all-slots-at-once, and keyword CASE -- because PR #916 pinned a reader in one container without varying the newline and #962 slipped through it. `execute()` runs `parse()` and never `check()`, so the runtime holds its own copy of these rules. The final `print 1` / `print 2` stay two instructions and two prints, pinning that a newline still terminates a statement -- the risk any newline-skipping fix carries. The reader operates on a dict, so Heritage also needs Data (`spec/conformance.md:305`).",
